COMMANDER_WEEKS_DEF.append((
    "Week 3: The Offices of Marshal and Seneschal",
    "The Marshal and Seneschal are the Commander's two principal staff officers. This week examines their distinct functions, how the Commander uses each office effectively, and the risks of conflating or neglecting either role.",
    _commander_reading(),
    ["Marshal", "Seneschal", "staff functions", "command delegation", "office distinction"],
    [
        q("A Commander who assigns both operational coordination and administrative compliance to the Marshal has:",
          "Simplified the command structure for greater efficiency",
          "Conflated the Marshal and Seneschal functions, likely creating overload and gaps in one area",
          "Made a reasonable adjustment given the Seneschal's inexperience",
          "Demonstrated best practice for lean command teams",
          "B",
          "The Marshal and Seneschal are distinct offices for a reason. Conflating them risks both operational overload and administrative neglect — the Commander's job is to deploy each office within its proper function."),
        q("The Seneschal's primary function in the Commander's staff is best described as:",
          "Leading training exercises and officer development programmes",
          "Overseeing administrative compliance, records, and the internal governance of the command",
          "Managing external relationships with other Orders and civil authorities",
          "Serving as the Commander's personal spokesman in public settings",
          "B",
          "The Seneschal is the administrative conscience of the command — responsible for records, compliance, and governance integrity. This is distinct from the Marshal's operational coordination role."),
        q("A Commander notices that the Marshal is making decisions that fall within the Seneschal's remit without consulting him. The correct response is to:",
          "Allow it since the Marshal is more senior and experienced",
          "Clearly re-establish each officer's scope and enforce the distinction going forward",
          "Reassign one of the offices to a single individual to prevent overlap",
          "Refer the matter to the Grand Master since it is an internal officer dispute",
          "B",
          "Office integrity requires that the Commander actively maintain role distinctions. Allowing the Marshal to encroach on Seneschal responsibilities creates confusion, resentment, and governance gaps."),
        q("In a time-sensitive operational crisis, the Commander delegates execution authority to the Marshal. This is appropriate when:",
          "The Commander is unavailable and wants to avoid any decision being made",
          "The matter falls squarely within the Marshal's operational function and the Commander has set clear parameters",
          "The Seneschal disagrees with the operational approach and needs to be bypassed",
          "The Commander wants to test the Marshal's decision-making under pressure",
          "B",
          "Delegating to the Marshal is appropriate and correct when the matter is operational and parameters are clear. Proper delegation is not abandonment — the Commander retains accountability and must set scope."),
        q("A Seneschal flags a compliance gap that the Marshal believes is unimportant operationally. The Commander should:",
          "Side with the Marshal since operational continuity is the priority",
          "Treat the Seneschal's concern as substantive — governance gaps matter regardless of operational inconvenience",
          "Ask them to resolve it between themselves",
          "Table the matter until the next formal review",
          "B",
          "Governance compliance is not optional when inconvenient. The Commander must take the Seneschal's compliance concerns seriously, because administrative gaps often become institutional vulnerabilities — especially during audits or leadership transitions."),
        q("Which scenario best illustrates the Marshal's function?",
          "Reviewing chapter membership records for accuracy before an annual report",
          "Coordinating the logistics of a multi-chapter training exercise, including scheduling and officer deployment",
          "Drafting the Commander's official correspondence to the Grand Master",
          "Maintaining the minutes of the Commander's staff meetings",
          "B",
          "The Marshal's function is operational coordination — scheduling, logistics, officer deployment across exercises and programmes. Record-keeping, correspondence, and minutes fall under Seneschal or administrative functions."),
        q("A Commander who rarely meets separately with the Seneschal and Marshal risks:",
          "Building strong team cohesion by treating both offices as equal",
          "Missing distinct intelligence from each office and inadvertently letting one dominate the other",
          "Creating an impression of favouritism toward neither officer",
          "Demonstrating appropriate hands-off leadership at the senior command level",
          "B",
          "Regular separate engagement with each staff officer ensures the Commander receives unfiltered input from both functions. Without this, one office tends to dominate briefings and the other's concerns go unheard."),
        q("The Marshal reports that a chapter's drill quality has degraded significantly. The Seneschal simultaneously reports that the same chapter is behind on required paperwork. The Commander should interpret this as:",
          "Two unrelated administrative issues to be handled separately through each office",
          "A systemic signal about the chapter's overall health that warrants direct Commander engagement",
          "A sign that both the Marshal and Seneschal are being overly critical",
          "An operational matter for the Marshal to resolve without further Commander involvement",
          "B",
          "Convergent signals from both the operational (Marshal) and administrative (Seneschal) domains indicate systemic chapter health problems. The Commander should treat this as requiring direct engagement rather than routing through separate offices independently."),
        q("Why does the KTKC maintain distinct Marshal and Seneschal offices rather than a single 'Chief of Staff'?",
          "Tradition requires two offices as a matter of historical form",
          "Operational and administrative functions require different mindsets, and separation prevents either from subordinating the other",
          "It doubles the number of senior staff positions to reward more officers",
          "A single officer could not be trusted with both sets of responsibilities",
          "B",
          "The structural separation of Marshal and Seneschal ensures that operational urgency does not consistently override administrative rigour, or vice versa. Both functions have equal standing and distinctive competencies."),
        q("A Commander preparing for a major transition (new officers being appointed) should prioritise which action involving the Seneschal?",
          "Having the Seneschal lead the farewell ceremonies for outgoing officers",
          "Ensuring the Seneschal conducts a full administrative handover audit before new officers take their positions",
          "Asking the Seneschal to informally brief the new officers on office culture",
          "Delegating transition planning entirely to the Marshal since it is an operational event",
          "B",
          "Transitions are prime moments for governance gaps. The Seneschal's role is to ensure records, compliance documentation, and administrative continuity are in order before new officers assume office — this protects the institution from inherited disorder."),
    ]
))

