COMMANDER_WEEKS_DEF.append((
    "Week 5: Authority, Judgment, and Command Presence",
    "Effective senior command requires more than positional authority — it requires cultivated judgment and visible command presence. This week examines how Commanders develop and exercise judgment under ambiguity, and what constitutes authentic command presence in the KTKC context.",
    _commander_reading(),
    ["command presence", "judgment under ambiguity", "positional authority", "cultivated authority", "command credibility"],
    [
        q("A Commander is asked to rule on an unusual situation not explicitly covered by existing statute. The best approach is to:",
          "Delay ruling until the Grand Master amends the statute",
          "Apply the spirit of existing doctrine and the mission to the situation, documenting the rationale for the record",
          "Rule in favour of whoever brought the matter since they clearly need support",
          "Declare the matter outside his jurisdiction and refuse to rule",
          "B",
          "Senior command judgment requires applying the spirit of doctrine to novel situations. Refusing to rule or demanding prior statutory coverage abdicates leadership. Documenting the rationale ensures accountability and provides precedent."),
        q("Command presence in the KTKC context is best defined as:",
          "Physical stature and a commanding voice that commands attention in ceremonial settings",
          "The consistent alignment of authority, conduct, and decision-making that gives others confidence the Commander can be trusted",
          "The Commander's skill at public speaking and formal address",
          "The rank insignia and ceremonial dress that visually signals the Commander's position",
          "B",
          "Command presence is relational and behavioral, not physical. It arises when the Commander's authority, personal conduct, and decisions consistently align — this alignment is what gives others confidence that the Commander can be trusted with senior responsibility."),
        q("A Commander faces a situation where the technically correct decision will cause short-term pain for a respected chapter. Judgment requires him to:",
          "Soften the decision to reduce the pain and maintain relationships",
          "Make the correct decision, explain the rationale clearly, and manage the relationship through the process",
          "Defer the decision indefinitely to avoid the conflict",
          "Let the Major handle it to insulate the Commander from the consequences",
          "B",
          "Senior command judgment means making the correct decision even when it is painful, while managing the relational consequences with honesty and care. Softening or deferring a correct decision to avoid short-term pain is a failure of judgment."),
        q("A Commander who consistently defers difficult decisions upward to the Grand Master is demonstrating:",
          "Appropriate humility and respect for the chain of command",
          "A failure of senior command judgment — decisions within his mandate must be made at his level",
          "Best practice for preventing unilateral mistakes at the senior level",
          "Effective use of the escalation process",
          "B",
          "Consistently escalating decisions that belong at the Commander level is an abdication of senior command responsibility. The Grand Master appointed the Commander to exercise judgment within his mandate, not to route every difficult call upward."),
        q("What is the primary risk of a Commander who relies exclusively on positional authority rather than cultivated authority?",
          "He will be respected too much to receive honest feedback",
          "Compliance will be surface-level and will evaporate when he is absent or challenged",
          "Officers will defer excessively and never develop their own initiative",
          "He will become too focused on ceremony rather than operations",
          "B",
          "Positional authority produces compliance without commitment — people follow the rank, not the person. When the Commander is absent, challenged, or pressured, surface compliance collapses. Cultivated authority earns genuine commitment."),
        q("A Commander who publicly admits a past decision was wrong and explains what he learned is demonstrating:",
          "Weakness that will undermine confidence in his authority",
          "Command credibility — authentic authority includes the capacity to correct course and model intellectual honesty",
          "Political skill in managing his public image",
          "A need for mentorship from the Grand Master",
          "B",
          "Admitting error and naming the learning is a mark of senior command credibility. Officers who model intellectual honesty build deeper trust than those who project infallibility. This is a component of cultivated authority."),
        q("During a crisis, a Commander remains calm and methodical while subordinate officers are agitated. This behaviour primarily functions to:",
          "Signal to subordinates that the Commander is emotionally detached",
          "Regulate the command environment — the Commander's composure sets the emotional ceiling for the entire group",
          "Buy time for the Commander to secretly consult the Grand Master",
          "Demonstrate that the crisis is not serious",
          "B",
          "Senior command presence includes emotional regulation under pressure. The Commander's composure sets the de facto emotional ceiling for the command — when he is calm, the group can function; when he panics, the group fragments."),
        q("A Commander makes a judgment call that turns out to be wrong. The most important thing he must do next is:",
          "Find the subordinate officer whose advice he followed and hold them accountable",
          "Own the decision, assess what led to the error, correct the course, and absorb the accountability",
          "Attribute the error to incomplete information provided by the Marshal",
          "Wait to see if the consequences are noticed before taking any action",
          "B",
          "Command accountability means owning decisions — including wrong ones. Deflecting blame onto subordinates or circumstances while one holds authority is a fundamental failure of command character."),
        q("Which scenario best illustrates genuine command judgment rather than rule-following?",
          "A Commander enforces a regulation even when it clearly does not fit the unusual situation at hand",
          "A Commander applies the principles behind a regulation to a novel situation the regulation did not anticipate",
          "A Commander refuses to act until he receives written instructions covering the exact scenario",
          "A Commander defers to the chapter's own judgment to avoid overstepping",
          "B",
          "Rule-following applies established rules mechanically. Genuine command judgment applies the principles behind rules to situations the rules did not anticipate — this is what distinguishes a commander from an administrator."),
        q("Command presence is undermined most directly when a Commander:",
          "Makes unpopular decisions that some officers disagree with",
          "Behaves differently in private than in public, creating inconsistency between visible authority and private character",
          "Insists on formal address and proper ceremonies at official events",
          "Holds high standards for officer conduct",
          "B",
          "Command presence depends on consistency between public authority and private character. When the two diverge — when the Commander behaves very differently when not observed — the cultivated authority he appears to hold dissolves."),
    ]
))

